data.content:
In .NET 10, `Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi` upgraded from **OpenAPI.NET v1** to **v2** (`Microsoft.OpenApi 2.x`). This is a major breaking change in the underlying model. Here's what changed and how to fix each issue:

---

## 1. `OpenApiString` / `OpenApiAny` removed

The `Microsoft.OpenApi.Any` namespace is gone. Schema examples are now `JsonNode`:

```csharp
using System.Text.Json.Nodes;

param.Schema.Example = JsonValue.Create("1.0");
```

---

## 2. `OpenApiResponse` → inline construction changed

`OpenApiResponses` no longer inherits from `Dictionary`. Use the new API:

```csharp
operation.Responses ??= new Dictionary<string, OpenApiResponse>();
operation.Responses.TryAdd("401", new OpenApiResponse { Description = "Unauthorized" });
```

---

## 3. `OpenApiReference` / `OpenApiSecurityScheme` changes

`ReferenceType` moved, and security schemes are referenced differently in v2:

```csharp
var oAuthScheme = new OpenApiSecurityScheme
{
    Reference = new OpenApiReference { Id = "oauth2" }
};
```

`ReferenceType` is no longer set manually — it's inferred from context, or you use the `Type` property with the new enum `ReferenceType.SecurityScheme` (check it still compiles; in v2 it may be `ReferenceType.SecurityScheme` under `Microsoft.OpenApi.Models.References`). If it doesn't resolve, use:

```csharp
using Microsoft.OpenApi.Models.References;

var oAuthScheme = new OpenApiSecuritySchemeReference("oauth2");
```

---

## 4. `Nullable` property removed from schema

OpenAPI v3.1 (which v2 targets) uses JSON Schema semantics — `nullable` is replaced by a `type` array including `"null"`:

```csharp
// Before
property.Value.Nullable = false;

// After — no-op for "not nullable" (default).
// To make something nullable:
// property.Value.Type = JsonSchemaType.String | JsonSchemaType.Null;
```

If you were explicitly setting `Nullable = false`, you can simply remove that code — it's the default.

---

## Summary of fixes

```csharp
using System.Text.Json.Nodes;
using Microsoft.OpenApi.Models;
using Microsoft.OpenApi.Models.References;

options.AddOperationTransformer((operation, context, ct) =>
{
    var param = operation.Parameters.FirstOrDefault(p => p.Name == "api-version");
    if (param is not null)
    {
        param.Schema.Example = JsonValue.Create("1.0");
    }

    operation.Responses ??= new Dictionary<string, OpenApiResponse>();
    operation.Responses.TryAdd("401", new OpenApiResponse { Description = "Unauthorized" });

    var oAuthScheme = new OpenApiSecuritySchemeReference("oauth2");
    operation.Security = new List<OpenApiSecurityRequirement>
    {
        new() { [oAuthScheme] = scopes }
    };

    return Task.CompletedTask;
});

options.AddSchemaTransformer((schema, context, ct) =>
{
    // Nullable property no longer exists; remove or replace with
    // type-array logic if you need to enforce nullability.
    return Task.CompletedTask;
});
```

Key takeaway: OpenAPI.NET v2 aligns with **OpenAPI 3.1 / JSON Schema**, so primitives become `JsonNode`, `Nullable` becomes a type union, and references use typed `*Reference` classes.
